{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And Terah died in Haran.</b> When Abram departed, there remained many years of Terah's life. Why did Scripture precede the account of Terah's death to the account of Abram's departure? So that the matter would not be publicized to all and they would say that Abram did not fulfill the honor of father and mother, for he left him old and went on his way — therefore it called him dead. Moreover, even the wicked in their lifetimes are called dead — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 11:32), and it is in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 39:7).\n\nAnd I am astonished at their words, for this is the way of all Scripture: to recount the life of the father and the son he begot and his death, and afterward to begin with the matter of the son. In all the generations the Scriptures followed this practice, and Noah himself — behold, in the days of Abram he was still alive, and Shem his son was alive all the days of Abraham. It is possible that they arrived at this midrash because Scripture set out the entire sequence of the passage for Terah in order, for in the case of Shem and all their generations Scripture did not mention death, nor did it go back and tally the years, whereas here it returned and took up the original order used for those of many years from Adam to Noah, and tallied: \"And all the days of Terah were… and he died,\" and further added the place of death, which was in Haran — the place it had mentioned in connection with Abraham. Therefore they expounded that all this was so that it would be evident without any discrepancy that Abraham was there with him. Moreover, since it had already begun with the matter of Abraham and related that he went forth with his father from Ur of the Chaldees to go to the land of Canaan, it would be problematic in their eyes if it did not arrange his life and death in sequence and write it in its proper time.\n\nAnd that which they further said (Bereshit Rabbah, ibid.), \"At first you expound that the wicked in their lifetimes are called dead\" — this too is astonishing in my eyes, for they already expounded (Bereshit Rabbah 34:4) from the verse \"But you shall go to your fathers in peace\" (Genesis 15:15) the tidings that his father has a share in the World to Come. Perhaps their intent is that he repented at the time of death, but all his days were in wickedness and he was called dead. And the language of Rashi teaches you that Terah repented at the time of death. Or perhaps he has a share in the World to Come by virtue of his son, as the Sages said, and that is the good tidings — that he himself did not know until he was informed of it at the time He said to him, \"But you shall go to your fathers in peace.\" And so I found in the midrash (Leviticus Rabbah 4:2): all woods are fit for the altar except that of olive and that of vine, because oil and wine are brought near to the altar — the fruits saved the trees. And so we find with Abraham, that he saved Terah, as it is said: \"But you shall go to your fathers in peace.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The midrash citation לק\"ט is resolved as Leviticus Rabbah based on the content matching Leviticus Rabbah 4:2; the abbreviation likely stands for לקח טוב (Tanchuma Buber on Leviticus), but the passage parallels Leviticus Rabbah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:35.194974916Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}